## PART 1: RESEARCH FOUNDATION

### 1.1 Academic Foundation — Behavior Preservation Is Not Optional

The intellectual core of Refactron — *deterministic transformations that provably preserve behavior* — has a 33-year academic lineage that the LLM-coding-tool crowd is simply ignoring.

**William F. Opdyke's 1992 PhD thesis at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, "Refactoring Object-Oriented Frameworks"** (advisor: Ralph E. Johnson), is the foundational document. Opdyke defined refactoring as *behavior-preserving program transformation* and introduced the critical concept that every refactoring has **preconditions** that must be checked before application — "for the same set of input values, the resulting set of output values should be the same before and after the refactoring." He defined seven such properties (unique superclass, distinct class names, distinct member names, inherited member variables not redefined, compatible signatures, etc.) and proved most low-level refactorings are trivially behavior-preserving while a few have undecidable preconditions in general — for which conservative algorithms suffice. Read the full thesis at laputan.org/pub/papers/opdyke-thesis.pdf. This is the document that justifies Refactron's existence: **safe refactoring requires checking preconditions, not pattern-matching token sequences**.

**Don Roberts' 1999 PhD thesis "Practical Analysis for Refactoring" (UIUC)** extended Opdyke by automating low-level refactorings in the Smalltalk Refactoring Browser (Roberts, Brant, Johnson, "A refactoring tool for Smalltalk," *Theory and Practice of Object Systems* 3, 1997, pp. 253–263). **Tokuda and Batory later showed Opdyke's preconditions alone were insufficient to guarantee behavior preservation** for complex refactorings — a warning Refactron should heed by combining preconditions *with* the test-gate verification step.

**Mens and Tourwé's "Formalising Behaviour Preserving Program Transformations" (Springer LNCS, 2002)** introduces a graph-transformation formalism and is the canonical reference for proving correctness of refactorings.

**Martin Fowler's *Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code* (Addison-Wesley, 1999; 2nd ed. 2018)** is the practitioner's bible and where the catalog of named transformations (Extract Method, Inline Variable, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism) lives.

**SQALE methodology — Jean-Louis Letouzey, "The SQALE method for evaluating Technical Debt" (3rd International Workshop on Managing Technical Debt @ ICSE, IEEE 2012, ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6225997)** — defines the Software Quality Assessment based on Lifecycle Expectations model: every quality violation has a **remediation cost** in minutes/hours, aggregated to a SQALE Index (total technical debt). ISO 9126-compliant. This is how SonarQube quantifies tech debt and is the right model for Refactron's "deep analysis" output: every detected pattern gets a remediation cost estimate.

**"On Preserving the Behavior in Software Refactoring: A Systematic Mapping Study" (arXiv:2106.13900)** surveys 142 primary studies and confirms that behavior preservation remains an open problem — most tools rely on testing as the empirical safety net rather than formal proof. This justifies Refactron's test-suite gate.

**Kaiyuan Wang et al., "Towards Refactoring-Aware Regression Test Selection" (ICSE 2018, ACM 10.1145/3180155.3180254)** — empirically, **only 22% of refactored methods/fields are covered by existing regression tests, yet refactorings are involved in nearly half of failed test cases.** Implication for Refactron: do not blindly trust the existing test suite as a complete oracle; report coverage of the changed surface so users can make informed decisions.

**Mongiovi, Gheyi, Soares, Teixeira, Borba, "Making Refactoring Safer Through Impact Analysis" (*Science of Computer Programming* 93, 2014, pp. 39–64)** is the canonical impact-analysis paper to cite when designing Refactron's dependency-graph step.

**Tree-sitter — Max Brunsfeld (GitHub, ex-Atom team), "Tree-sitter: a new parsing system for programming tools" (Strange Loop 2018)** is the engineering talk that explains why tree-sitter exists: a uniform C API, incremental parsing, robust error recovery on incomplete code, and a built-in S-expression query system. The design draws on Tim A. Wagner's "Practical Algorithms for Incremental Software Development Environments" (UC Berkeley PhD, 1998). Tree-sitter is the right parser for Refactron's deep-analysis step because it handles partially-broken legacy code without crashing.

### 1.2 Industry Precedents — How Meta, Google, Instagram Already Did Most of This

**Meta / jscodeshift** (github.com/facebook/jscodeshift) — created at Facebook in 2015, written in JavaScript, built on top of *recast* (preserves formatting) and *ast-types* (AST traversal/manipulation), parses with Babel/Babylon/Flow/TS. Used internally for thousands of React migrations. jQuery-style API: `j(source).findJSXElements('Button').forEach(path => ...)`. **Critical lesson for Refactron**: jscodeshift was abandoned by Meta — "There are currently no active maintainers of jscodeshift at Meta" (Issue #587, August 2024). Maintenance was handed to the Codemod team. The 9-year gap between v0.x and v17.0.0 (August 2024) tells you this is unloved infrastructure. **There is a real market gap for a maintained, opinionated, safety-first successor.**

**Google / ClangMR** — Wright, Jasper et al., "Large-Scale Automated Refactoring Using ClangMR" (research.google/pubs/large-scale-automated-refactoring-using-clangmr/, ICSME 2013). Combines the Clang compiler framework with MapReduce to refactor Google's C++ codebase at scale. The paper explicitly notes that "automatically finding and transforming code in a semantically correct way can be challenging, particularly as the size of a codebase increases" — and that **Google chose semantic, type-attributed transformation over text-based find/replace**. This is the precedent for OpenRewrite's LST design. Google also runs **Rosie** internally for large-scale C++ changes (referenced in *Software Engineering at Google*).

**Instagram / LibCST** (github.com/Instagram/LibCST) — Benjamin Woodruff, "Static Analysis at Scale: An Instagram Story" (instagram-engineering.com). The key engineering insight: Python's standard `ast` module is **lossy** (drops comments, parens, whitespace), so codemods that re-emit code mangle formatting. LibCST is a **Concrete Syntax Tree** that "creates a compromise between an AST and a traditional CST" — it preserves all formatting details while exposing AST-like semantics. Built on top of Guido van Rossum's `pgen2` (originally for `lib2to3`) and David Halter's `parso`. Ships with codemods like `ConvertFormatStringCommand` (converts `.format()` to f-strings) — **the exact transform Refactron has planned.** This is your reference implementation; do not reinvent it.

**Adam Stepinski's "Refactoring a Python Codebase with LibCST" (engineering.instawork.com)** is the best engineering case study: walks through identifying nodes, writing visitors, handling false positives, the visitor pattern, and unit-testing codemods. Required reading before Week 4.

**OpenRewrite (Moderne)** (docs.openrewrite.org) — the gold-standard large-scale automated refactoring framework, mostly Java but expanding to JS/TS/Python. The key concept is the **Lossless Semantic Tree (LST)**: type-attributed (every node carries fully-qualified type info, even for types defined in other files/transitive deps) AND format-preserving (whitespace, indentation, line breaks reproduced exactly). 5,000+ community recipes. **Recipes are deterministic programs that navigate the LST** — exactly Refactron's positioning. Moderne's 2026 Java Code Geeks piece ("OpenRewrite: The Automated Migration Tool That's Quietly Changing How Teams Upgrade Java") explains why LSTs beat ASTs for refactoring output quality.

**Comby (comby.dev)** — Rijnard van Tonder's structural code search and replace, language-agnostic via lightweight templates with holes `:[name]`. Underneath, Comby "uses no tree definition, but turns patterns into an executable routine (a language-aware parser) where the tree structure is implicit." Trade-off: more flexible patterns, less semantic precision than ts-morph or LibCST. Good for one-off fixes, not deep refactors. Powers Sourcegraph's structural search.

**Semgrep autofix** (semgrep.dev/docs/writing-rules/autofix) — r2c's rule-based code transformation, evolved from Coccinelle/Spatch (Yoann Padioleau, originally for Linux kernel collateral evolutions). Semgrep's blog post "Powerfully autofixing code with Semgrep's new AST-based approach" (2022) reports **AST-based autofix succeeds in 96.4% of Python test cases and 100.0% of JavaScript test cases** from `semgrep-rules` — but only because they limit scope to expressions and recycle original text where possible. Lesson: deterministic AST refactoring at high accuracy *is achievable* if you scope tightly.

**GitHub / tree-sitter** — Brunsfeld's tree-sitter now powers in-browser symbolic code navigation on GitHub.com. Bindings exist for Go, Haskell, Java, JS (Node + WASM), Kotlin, Python, Ruby, Rust, Swift, Zig.

**JetBrains Structural Search and Replace (SSR)** — IntelliJ's pattern-based code transformation; included in every JetBrains IDE. Less famous than jscodeshift but battle-tested across millions of users.

**Babel** (babeljs.io) is the de-facto AST transformation pipeline for JS, with the visitor pattern that every codemod author internalizes. **AST Explorer (astexplorer.net)** by Felix Kling is where engineers iterate on transforms — Refactron should ship a "paste your code here" web playground in Phase 2.

### 1.3 Technical Stack Research

| Tool | Language | Strength | Weakness | Role in Refactron |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **tree-sitter** | C + bindings | Incremental, error-recovering, multi-language | No type info | Step 1: Deep Analysis parsing |
| **ts-morph** (dsherret) | TypeScript | Wraps TS Compiler API, full type checker, "easier than raw compiler API" | TS/JS only | Step 2: TS Refactoring Engine |
| **LibCST** | Python | Lossless CST, batchable visitor, mature codemod tooling | Python only, slower than `ast` | Step 2: Python Refactoring Engine |
| **jscodeshift** | JS/TS | Mature, recast-based, jQuery API | Unmaintained at Meta | Reference impl only — do NOT depend |
| **recast / ast-types** | JS | Format-preserving | Lower-level | Fallback if ts-morph too heavy |
| **Comby** | Polyglot | Language-agnostic patterns | Imprecise semantics | NOT used in Refactron MVP |
| **Semgrep** | Polyglot | YAML rules, autofix, taint | Not deep refactor | Inspiration for rule format |

**ts-morph specifics** (ts-morph.com, npmjs.com/package/ts-morph): "Wraps the compiler API objects to provide helper methods for getting information and programmatically changing files. Allows falling back to the compiler API objects if necessary (e.g., `classDeclaration.compilerNode` or `typeChecker.compilerObject`). All changes are kept in memory (including file and directory moves) until specifying to save to the underlying file system." Maintained by David Sherret. **Warning from the docs:** "When manipulating the AST via this library, the underlying TypeScript AST tree is regenerated each time. For this reason, it's important not to hold on to TypeScript compiler nodes between manipulations." Plan transforms as pure functions of the current tree state.

**Atomic file writes** — use npm's `write-file-atomic` (github.com/npm/write-file-atomic), maintained by the npm team: writes to `filename + "." + murmurhex(__filename, process.pid, ++invocations)`, fsyncs, then `fs.rename`s into place. POSIX `rename(2)` is atomic on the same filesystem. This is the standard pattern Refactron's atomic-write step should adopt verbatim — do not reinvent.

### 1.4 Market Validation — Real Numbers on the Pain

**Stripe / Harris Poll, "The Developer Coefficient" (September 2018, stripe.com/files/reports/the-developer-coefficient.pdf)** — surveyed thousands of C-level executives and developers across 30+ industries:
- Developers spend **17.3 hours per week on maintenance** (dealing with bad code, debugging, refactoring, modifying) vs. 41.1 hours total — i.e., **42% of work time** on tech debt.
- 13.5 hours on tech debt specifically; another ~4 hours on "bad code."
- "Bad code" costs companies **$85 billion annually**; tech debt has a **$3 trillion impact on global GDP** over 10 years.
- This 33% maintenance-burden number is the citation behind every "tech debt is real" pitch deck.

**Stack Overflow 2024 Developer Survey (survey.stackoverflow.co/2024, 65,000+ respondents from 185 countries)** — **technical debt is the #1 frustration for 62% of professional developers** (some interview tracks cite 63%), twice the rate of the #2 and #3 frustrations (complex tech stacks for building/deployment). Improving code/database quality is the **#1 source of satisfaction** for all three segments (happy, complacent, unhappy). Translation: developers actively *want* to pay down debt but lack tools that let them do it safely. Refactron's wedge.

**AI Code Failure Rates** — the data validates the "no LLM" moat:
- **NYU study of 1,692 GitHub Copilot-generated programs: ~40% contained exploitable security vulnerabilities.** C code showed ~50% vulnerability rates; Python 39%; Java 72% with XSS failing 86% of the time. Cited across LLM-evaluation literature.
- **University of Texas at San Antonio / Virginia Tech / University of Oklahoma joint study (16 LLMs, 576,000 code samples): 19.7% of recommended packages were fabricated and non-existent** (205,000 hallucinated packages); 58% repeated across queries, enabling supply-chain attacks ("slopsquatting").
- **ACM/IEEE AST 2024, "Using GitHub Copilot for Test Generation in Python: An Empirical Study" (dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3644032.3644443): if you generate tests with Copilot without an existing test suite, 92.45% of the tests are failing, broken, or empty.**
- **Liu et al., "Beyond Functional Correctness: Exploring Hallucinations in LLM-Generated Code" (arXiv:2404.00971):** taxonomy of code hallucinations across CodeGen, CodeRL, Codex, ChatGPT — confirms hallucination is structural, not accidental.

**Industry framing:** Diffblue's "Precision over hallucination" piece distinguishes *assistive AI* (Copilot, Cursor — must be reviewed) from *autonomous AI* (must guarantee correctness). Refactron is in a third category they don't name: **deterministic non-AI tooling that produces autonomous, verified output**. That's the positioning.

